-**Parameters and Edge Cases:**
-- **Implementation Complexity:** High. It requires maintaining parent pointers and carefully distinguishing between internal and external transitions [S1].
-- **Memory Overhead:** None. It uses no additional memory structures beyond the tree itself [S1].
-- **Precision:** Care must be taken when $t_{exit}$ is very close to $t_{entry}$ to avoid infinite loops or skipping cells [G].
-- **Degenerate Input:** If the ray grazes a face, the exit face determination must be robust.
+**Parameters:**
+*   Implementation complexity: High. Described as the most complex of the three presented methods in terms of implementation [S1].
+*   Memory overhead: None [S1].
+*   Additional computations: None [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Root Handling:** The source does not specify behavior if the ascent reaches the root without finding an internal transition. [G] In practice, this may require restarting the traversal from the root or treating the root's exit as a special case.
+*   **Precision:** Floating-point precision errors in `point_in_aabb` checks may lead to incorrect classification of internal vs. external transitions. [G] Epsilon comparisons should be used.
+*   **Degenerate Input:** Rays tangent to cell walls may cause ambiguous transition classifications. [G] Robust intersection tests are required.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Average case is $O(1)$ per voxel transition, similar to standard grid traversal, but with higher constant factors due to parent pointer dereferencing and ascent/descent logic. Worst case is $O(D)$ where $D$ is the depth of the tree, if the ray traverses a path requiring frequent ascents and descents.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space.
-- **Strengths:** No memory overhead; avoids the $O(D)$ cost of restarting from the root for every step, which is beneficial for sparse octrees where many transitions are between siblings [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Higher implementation complexity compared to simple top-down or two-pass methods [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the tree depth and the number of ascents/descents. In the best case (internal transitions), it is $O(1)$ per step (sibling lookup). In the worst case, it may require $O(D)$ ascents and $O(D)$ descents, where $D$ is the tree depth.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory, as it uses the existing tree structure and pointers [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** No memory overhead, no additional computations, applicable to regular and barycentric octree grids [S1]. Avoids the overhead of root-to-leaf searches for non-internal transitions [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** High implementation complexity [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When memory is constrained and implementation complexity is acceptable. Suitable for regular and barycentric octree grids [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When implementation simplicity is prioritized over traversal efficiency.
 
 ## Background
-This method was proposed in the context of Monte Carlo radiative transfer simulations using hierarchical octrees [S1]. It was introduced as an alternative to the standard top-down method (which restarts from the root) and the two-pass method, aiming to balance performance and implementation simplicity [S1]. It is applicable to both regular and barycentric octree grids [S1].
+*   **Origin:** The method is described in the context of Monte Carlo radiative transfer simulations using hierarchical octrees [S1].
+*   **History:** It is presented as an alternative to the standard top-down method and a two-pass hierarchy traversal method [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Used in 3D Monte Carlo radiative transfer simulations [S1].
 
